import{_ as i,P as n,d as a,e as o,y as s,q as r,f as c}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const b=JSON.parse(`{"title":"A.2 Boundaries and Interfaces (HV02)","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"A.2 Boundaries and Interfaces (HV02)","xkChars":0,"xkWords":1013,"xkLessons":{"A.2":{"title":"A.2 Boundaries and Interfaces (HV02)","lead":"The human variable “Boundaries and Interfaces” separates formal boundaries, actual passage, and causal effects of specified channels; neither membership nor influence scope automatically becomes consent or jurisdiction.","explanation":["Original location: Chinese source lines 3866–3949; original site location /theory/appendix-a/a-02#section-a-2. See the theory-v2 source for the current full text; historical research manuscripts and excerpts remain available for download and checking against the original edition.","Short quotation: “membership status amounts to consent” belongs under “Cannot infer.” This variable does not merely draw an inside/outside line; it records members, resources, information, rights, responsibilities, and cross-boundary interfaces together. Formal rosters, actual resource control, technical openness, and real passage may differ; relevant judgments should pause when these are conflated or exclusions are invisible. [T-main]","The upstream inferential premise is “Structural Domain,” but inputs may be candidate objects given by that variable or supported structural domains, carrying their evidence status. Route one does not require a grouping-gain test to have passed in advance, so “grouping gain has not yet been established” does not prevent describing thresholds, waiting, and refusals; nor does it permit silently rewriting a candidate object as a unified community. [T-main; T-HV01]","Route one (boundary inventory) permits description of boundary states, interface accessibility, gatekeeping positions, refusal records, and exclusion risks when the six boundary contents and formal–actual differences can be stated item by item. Its ceiling is inventory and description only; it cannot claim that the boundary has produced a causal selection effect. “Selectively open” as a state differs in strength from an established causal effect. [T-main]","Route two (selective effect) requires a pre-registered boundary or interface change that, through one qualified channel-effect test, shows an above-threshold channel effect on specified cross-boundary flows or admission/refusal outcomes, with the conditions for causal claims and “Retaining Competing Explanations and Residuals” further required. The conclusions it can draw are limited to the specified channel, time window, positions, and distribution of cross-boundary costs; spatial, organizational, or influence scope cannot imply authority to act on the axis of jurisdiction and authorization. Observed before-and-after change still requires addressing competing explanations and matched comparisons. [T-main; T-G2]","A boundary is not closure. An interface may be open to some information, impose eligibility restrictions on other resources, and have different actual accessibility for different groups; boundaries of rights and responsibilities need not coincide with physical walls. Moving to a larger scale on the nine axes requires adding membership categories, cross-domain interfaces, representative authorization, and inherited protections; especially after gatekeeping nodes change, recheck who can refuse and who can appeal. [T-main]","States, observables, and evidence jointly help locate gaps: roster exceptions, usage records, flows, refusal reasons, waiting times, and formal–actual differences each answer different questions. For positions unable to access, unable to exit, or withheld for protection, “no usage record” cannot imply absence of need or existing consent. [T-main]","Outputs connect to uptake in the sense of the action uptake layer, write-back in the sense of feedback write-back, and exit in the sense of the protection-floor item “Genuine Exit and Alternative Uptake,” but the existence of an interface does not establish arrival of resources, write-back of bad news, or safe exit. Authorized restoration must correct membership/refusal records, actually restore corresponding admission or interface states, revoke erroneous actions, and verify completion; merely changing “closed” to “open” in a table is insufficient. [T-main]","The OECD’s urban accessibility method distinguishes the presence of a stop from accessibility of service opportunities, providing a local reference for the difference between formal openness and actual accessibility; it has not performed a pre-registered channel-effect test here, and a methodological definition cannot count as causal support for a boundary change. The items of the source text are preserved in full below, with interpretation limited by the selected route. [P-OECD; T-main]","Item-by-item study (“Source text” below retains the item’s complete content; “Interpretation” specifies its role and irreplaceable responsibility).","01 | Code. The source text gives the code only in parentheses at the end of the heading and uses the name in the text. Explanation: the code is a short identifier within the code system of Appendix B, tracking the same variable; it does not indicate that a real object has been established. [T-main]","02 | Qualified code. The source text does not list this item separately. Explanation: the qualified form serves only to avoid name collisions; references to this variable must still carry the object and evidence status. [T-main]","03 | Name. Source heading: Boundaries and Interfaces. Explanation: the name provides a reading entry point, not an automatic label for specific people, groups, or real structures. [T-main]","04 | Proposition role. The source text does not list this item separately; under §2.2 it belongs to the role of “human-domain clauses.” Interpretation: this marks it as a proposition of the human domain; it cannot be upgraded into formal proof or an unconditional law of the world. [T-main]","05 | Role in the system. The source text lists it as one of the eleven human variables of Part 7 (§7.3). Interpretation: this is the definition of a human variable, specifying how it enters analysis; listing the items does not establish that any empirical test holds. [T-main]","06 | Proposition. Source text: For human boundaries, members, resources, information, rights, responsibilities, and cross-boundary interfaces must be stated together. Explanation: this proposition must be understood together with the support routes and ceilings below; unconditional causal or normative conclusions cannot be extracted from one sentence. [T-main]","07 | Scope of application. Source text: Human structures with inclusion, exclusion, exchange, or jurisdiction. Explanation: the scope lists task domains that may be examined; it does not mean all objects within it have empirical support. [T-main]","08 | Conditions for suspension. Source text: Formal and actual boundaries are conflated, or what is excluded is invisible. Explanation: pausing applies to judgments or adoption dependent on this; independently established descriptions may remain, but invalid premises cannot continue to be used. [T-main]","09 | Inferential premises. Source text: Structural domain (§7.3.1). Explanation: a reference to “Structural Domain” may carry candidate status; this variable’s route one does not require a grouping-gain test to have passed. An upstream candidate reference must not be elevated by concealing its evidence status. [T-main]","10 | Method requirements. Source text: Constraint on Object Declaration (§4.8.1); conditions for evidence (§2.5); conditions for sources (§2.7). Interpretation: method requirements constrain evidence gathering and expression; meeting method requirements does not independently prove an empirical proposition true. [T-main]","11 | Domain restriction. Source text: None. Interpretation: writing “none” means only that no such domain restriction is listed, not that evidential responsibility is absent; a listed restriction also does not imply universal validity. [T-main]","12 | Objects of application. Source text: None. Interpretation: objects of application cannot replace the current object’s definition; writing “none” is an explicit statement, not permission to apply to every object. [T-main]","13 | Support routes. Source text: Route one, boundary inventory. Conclusion level: Descriptive classification. Conditions: Members, resources, information, rights, responsibilities, cross-boundary interfaces, and the differences between formal and actual boundaries can be stated item by item. Further inferential premises: None. Further method requirements: None. Can conclude: Describe boundary states, interface accessibility, gatekeeping positions, records of refusal, and exclusion risks. Ceiling: An inventory of boundaries and interfaces only. It must not be asserted that the boundary has produced a causal selective effect. Route two, selective effect. Conclusion level: Conditional effect. Conditions: An eligible channel-effect test (§4.4) shows that a pre-registered change to a boundary or interface has a channel effect above the threshold on the specified outcomes for cross-boundary flow, admission, or refusal. Further inferential premises: This channel-effect test. Further method requirements: Conditions for causal claims (§2.4); Retaining Competing Explanations and Residuals (§4.8.4). Can conclude: Determine the boundary’s selective effect and the distribution of cross-boundary costs for the specified channel, time window, and position. Ceiling: Limited to tested channels and outcomes. Jurisdiction and authority to act on the axis of jurisdiction and authorization must not be inferred from spatial extent, organizational scope, or scope of influence. Explanation: route one only inventories boundaries and interfaces; route two requires a qualified pre-registered channel-effect test, and further requires the conditions for causal claims and “Retaining Competing Explanations and Residuals.” Sharing a task object does not give the two routes equal conclusion strength. [T-main]","14 | Can infer. Source text: Boundary selectivity; interface accessibility; cross-boundary costs. Explanation: boundary selectivity, accessibility, and costs may be described under route one; a claim of causal effect for a specified channel requires route two, and this short list alone cannot bypass the routes. [T-main]","15 | Cannot infer. Source text: That a boundary amounts to closure; that membership status amounts to consent; that scope of influence amounts to jurisdiction. Explanation: these are common overreaches that cannot be avoided merely by rephrasing; check actual inference and subsequent use, not wording alone. [T-main]","16 | Nine-axis scale. Source text: Aggregation level covers individual cross-boundary events, cases of interface use, populations by membership category, distributions of admission and refusal, and aggregation rules. Spatial extent covers physical points of entry, organizational boundaries, digital interfaces, legal jurisdictions, and cross-domain channels. Time span covers the period when a boundary is in force, waiting at interfaces, migration lags, and cycles of reorganization. Organizational level covers user roles, gatekeeping teams, organizations, institutions, and governance ecologies. Causal level covers cross-boundary events, mechanisms of gatekeeping interaction, interface structure, institutional rules, and systemic conditions. Observation resolution covers original admission and refusal records, sequences of use, individual cases, flow distributions, service metrics, and summaries. Record compression loss as well. Scope of influence covers direct users, people excluded, positions where benefits or costs occur indirectly, second-order spillovers, and impacts across domains and generations. Network topology scope covers interface nodes, gatekeeping bottlenecks, alternative paths, and cross-domain connections. Scope of jurisdiction and authorization states the authorization for inclusion, exclusion, interface changes, and adjustments of rights and responsibilities separately. When moving to a larger scale, the differences must be recorded axis by axis. Explanation: state all nine axes individually; in particular, scope of influence does not produce permission on the axis of jurisdiction and authorization, and compression losses must be retained when observation resolution decreases. [T-main]","17 | Effective object. Source text: A boundary that selectively affects flows of resources, information, rights, or responsibilities. Explanation: the source text describes a boundary that selectively affects flows; under route one, treat it as an object to describe or test, without using the name to declare a causal selection effect in advance. [T-main]","18 | Kept across scales. Source text: Positions inside and outside; cross-boundary channels; limits of rights and responsibilities. Explanation: what is kept across scales is a comparative responsibility to preserve during transfer, not a claim that variable values remain unchanged at every scale. [T-main]","19 | Must be added when moving to a larger scale. Source text: New membership categories; interfaces across domains; representation and authorization; carryover of protections. Explanation: moving to a larger scale is not copying and enlarging the original table. When materials about new populations, interfaces, responsibility positions, and so on are incomplete, limit conclusions dependent on them. [T-main]","20 | Changes with scale. Source text: Membership, interfaces, and the boundaries of actual control can change. Explanation: the same word may change meaning when the object or scale changes; rebuild correspondences rather than assuming the original meaning applies unchanged. [T-main]","21 | Objects to which it does not apply. Source text: Nonhuman boundaries without concepts of membership, rights, or responsibility. Explanation: the non-applicability restriction prevents unconditional projection of human normative, responsibility, and experience concepts; it does not deny that other theories can study these objects. [T-main]","22 | Cannot be raised to. Source text: Jurisdiction arising automatically as soon as spatial extent or organizational scope expands. Explanation: inference from local to whole needs separate support; broad influence, grand names, or formal similarity cannot supply missing representativeness or authorization. [T-main]","23 | States. Source text: Open; selectively open; closed; disputed; reorganizing. Explanation: states are descriptive classifications requiring evidence, not a maturity ladder for scoring people; state changes require a time window, grounds, and a record of changes. [T-main]","24 | Observables. Source text: Membership eligibility and decisions on admission and exit; cross-boundary flows of resources, information, rights, and responsibilities; gatekeeping nodes, wait times, and reasons for refusal; differences between formal boundaries and boundaries of actual passage. Explanation: observable leads indicate where to seek materials; a single lead is not proof of a complete mechanism or causal effect. [T-main]","25 | Evidence requirements. Source text: Membership lists and exceptions; records of interface use; records of cross-boundary flows and refusals. Explanation: obtain evidence according to route and task relevance; distinguish unknown, not yet collected, not applicable, and withheld for protection, without forcing disclosure to fill the table. [T-main]","26 | Inputs. Source text: The candidate object, or the supported structural domain, given by the variable “Structural Domain,” together with its evidential status; roles and authorization. Route one does not require a grouping-gain test (§4.3) to pass first. Explanation: this specifies what comes from upstream and its qualifications; gaps cannot silently disappear during transfer between variables. [T-main]","27 | Outputs. Source text: Uptake in the sense of the action uptake layer (§7.3.5); write-back in the sense of feedback write-back (§7.3.7); exit in the sense of the protection-floor item “Genuine Exit and Alternative Uptake” (§14.8). Explanation: outputs indicate which downstream judgments may depend on this variable; passing a reference does not guarantee downstream conclusions, actual repair, or authorization. [T-main]","28 | Time window and lag. Source text: Record lags in boundaries taking effect or changing and in exit and appeal. Explanation: occurrence, observation, execution, and arrival of effects may happen at different times; short-term materials cannot automatically certify extension beyond the window. [T-main]","29 | Uncertainty. Source text: Record informal boundaries, access through representatives, and drift in digital spaces. Explanation: tie uncertainty to specific materials and positions; a single overall confidence score must not conceal positions that were never covered. [T-main]","30 | Local exclusion zone. Source text: Positions without access to the interface, without an exit, or withheld for protection. Explanation: exclusion zones limit the coverage of current inferences; they do not erase these people or facts from the world, and safety-related unavailability does not mean consent or absence of problems. [T-main]","31 | Affected positions. Source text: Members; applicants; people excluded; people bearing burdens outside the boundary. Explanation: record both directly and indirectly affected people; visible members do not represent everyone bearing costs. [T-main]","32 | Uptake carriers. Source text: Membership rules; access mechanisms; legal or institutional boundaries; technical interfaces. Explanation: carriers are the structures or processes actually carrying something; the carrier’s name, completed construction, and delivery of required functions are different evidence. [T-main]","33 | Responsible parties. Source text: Boundary setters; interface operators; parties granting authorization. Explanation: specify who proposes the judgment and who operates or uses it; recording responsibility does not automatically adjudicate legal liability or issue new authorization. [T-main]","34 | Normative status. Source text: Facts about a boundary are kept separate from its normative justification. Explanation: distinguish descriptive, normative, and bridging status according to the detailed description. Established facts do not independently determine value rankings, and normative reasons do not automatically establish empirical effects. [T-main]","35 | Judgment ceiling. Source text: When evidence about interfaces and impacts is sufficient, judgments may go up to the level of diagnosis. Explanation: “When evidence about interfaces and impacts is sufficient, judgments may go up to the level of diagnosis” is an overall upper bound; route one still stops at inventory description, and diagnosis cannot issue permission to change admission or resource flows. [T-main]","36 | Action ceiling. Source text: This variable produces only descriptions of boundary states, interface barriers, exclusion risks, and testing needs. It does not authorize changes to admission, exit, rights, or resource flows. Any real-world adjustment must separately pass normative bridging (§4.14.1) and have in place the normative premises stated explicitly on this occasion, itemized authorization, and operational procedures. Explanation: the variable itself produces no real-world authority to act; actual actions require separate checks of choice, normative bridging, explicitly stated normative premises, valid itemized authorization, operational procedures, protections, and time limits. [T-main]","37 | Counterexamples. Source text: The formal membership boundary is the opposite of the boundary of actual resource control; a digital interface is open, but physical, language, or safety barriers prevent some positions from gaining access. Explanation: use counterexamples to limit unconditional extrapolation; they may require narrowing scope or collecting evidence again, but do not automatically invalidate every bounded use. [T-main]","38 | Appeal. Source text: Under the common rule for appeal and restoration (§7.4), affected people inside and outside the boundary can raise objections to inclusion, exclusion, and interface barriers through a safe, accessible channel with anti-retaliation protection, and initiate a review independent of the original decision chain for the boundary. Explanation: appeals must be safely accessible, free of retaliation, and able to trigger independent review; a name alone does not mean there is power to correct errors. [T-main]","39 | Restoration. Source text: Under the same rule, once restoration is authorized, the designated responsible party must within the stated deadline correct membership and refusal records, actually restore affected admission rights or interface states, and reverse erroneous boundary actions. Changes leave a record, and their completion is verified. Explanation: within valid authorization, actually revoke erroneous effects, correct downstream consequences, and verify completion; changing document wording does not mean rights and resources have been restored to the person concerned. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["Fix scope using a candidate or supported object given by “Structural Domain,” retaining its evidence status.","Record formal–actual differences item by item for members, resources, information, rights, responsibilities, and interfaces.","Route one first describes thresholds and exclusions; an inventory must not be treated as a causal effect.","Route two additionally uses a pre-registered channel-effect test, and tests specified channels and outcomes under the conditions for causal claims and “Retaining Competing Explanations and Residuals.”","When transferring scale, check new members, cross-domain interfaces, representative authority, and inherited protections.","Correct boundaries through safe independent appeals; once authorized, restore actual interfaces and downstream records."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Formal boundary","leftText":"How rules or rosters specify it.","rightTitle":"Actual boundary","rightText":"How resources and passage are controlled in practice."},{"leftTitle":"Inventory description (route one)","leftText":"Describes who can reach, who is denied, and costs.","rightTitle":"Channel effect (route two)","rightText":"A pre-registered channel-effect test supports the causal effect of a specified change."},{"leftTitle":"Membership","leftText":"Recorded in a particular member set.","rightTitle":"Consent","rightText":"Requires separate checking of the specific matter and genuine choice."},{"leftTitle":"Scope of influence","leftText":"Where a boundary change has effects.","rightTitle":"Scope of jurisdiction and authorization","rightText":"Determined by independently valid authorization."}],"boundaries":["Counterexample in the source text: the formal membership boundary is the opposite of the boundary of actual resource control.","Counterexample in the source text: a digital interface is open, but physical, language, or safety barriers prevent some positions from gaining access.","Teaching counterexample: an account’s existence is taken to imply consent to every use of personal data.","Boundary in the original: spatial expansion does not automatically add authority for inclusion, exclusion, or resource disposition."],"questions":["Can “Boundaries and Interfaces” be entered while “Structural Domain” has no grouping-gain support?","Does an open interface mean everyone can actually access it?","Does a change from 20% to 40% automatically support route two?","Is correcting only the roster sufficient for restoration?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-2/figures/hv02-routes.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Formal and actual differences in urban-access indicators","href":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/transport-bridging-divides_55ae1fd8-en/full-report/component-5.html"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Formal and actual differences in urban-access indicators","text":"OECD Chapter 2 notes that having transport stops does not guarantee access to sufficient opportunities, and distinguishes distance-based criteria from time/transport-mode criteria.","sourceLabel":"OECD: Transport Bridging Divides, Chapter 2","sourceUrl":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/transport-bridging-divides_55ae1fd8-en/full-report/component-5.html","sourceLocator":"Measuring access to opportunities; definitions of three indicators; web page lines 3869–3877","boundary":"Only a conceptual and measurement comparison for accessibility, not a channel-effect test of this variable or proof that an admission or jurisdictional arrangement is justified.","href":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/transport-bridging-divides_55ae1fd8-en/full-report/component-5.html"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"A.2","start_line":3923,"end_line":4006,"startLine":3923,"endLine":4006}],"outputId":"section-a-2","sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: an open URL and actual passage","text":"Everyone can open a fictional service’s webpage, but submission requires a particular credential and its sole supported language is incomprehensible to some users. Route one may list boundaries at webpage, eligibility, and language levels, and unknown affected positions. “Can open” cannot declare universal accessibility, nor can untested removal of a barrier be said to produce a particular effect.","assumptions":"A constructed service scenario, with no actual personal data or eligibility decisions.","boundary":"Describing barriers authorizes no admission changes; normative justification and causal effects require separate checks."},{"title":"Teaching construction: before/after figures are not automatically a channel effect","text":"Before a constructed interface change, 20 of 100 applications succeed; afterward, 40 of 100 succeed. The observed success proportion rises from 20% to 40%, a 20-percentage-point difference. If applicant composition, demand, or other processes changed simultaneously, the difference cannot be attributed to the interface alone. Applying for route two additionally requires an eligible pre-registered channel-effect test and rules for comparison, thresholds, time windows, and evidence; there is no formal “supported” status here.","assumptions":"The numbers are entirely instructional; two sets of 100 do not guarantee the same population or a valid comparison.","boundary":"Correlation or before/after differences cannot replace the conditions for causal claims; changing barriers also requires independent authority."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Counterexample in the source text: the formal membership boundary is the opposite of the boundary of actual resource control.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Counterexample in the source text: a digital interface is open, but physical, language, or safety barriers prevent some positions from gaining access.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: an account’s existence is taken to imply consent to every use of personal data.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the original: spatial expansion does not automatically add authority for inclusion, exclusion, or resource disposition.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Can “Boundaries and Interfaces” be entered while “Structural Domain” has no grouping-gain support?","answer":"A candidate-object reference may take route one while retaining boundaries and uncertainty.","basis":"The “Inputs” item and route one."},{"question":"Does an open interface mean everyone can actually access it?","answer":"No; check physical, language, safety, and eligibility barriers and actual interface records.","basis":"Counterexamples and the “Observables” item."},{"question":"Does a change from 20% to 40% automatically support route two?","answer":"No; before/after differences additionally require proportionate causal comparisons of the channel effect and pre-registration rules.","basis":"The conditions of route two and the conditions for causal claims."},{"question":"Is correcting only the roster sufficient for restoration?","answer":"Actually restore affected admission/interfaces within valid scope, correct records, and verify completion.","basis":"The “Restoration” item."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-A-2/figures/hv02-routes.png","alt":"Route one’s boundary inventories require no prior grouping-gain test; route two requires specified-channel effects supported by a channel-effect test, and further requires the conditions for causal claims and “Retaining Competing Explanations and Residuals”; neither route grants disposition authority.","caption":"Based on the complete support routes in A.2."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-A-2/figures/hv02-interfaces.png","alt":"Check formal and actual boundaries separately for cross-boundary interfaces of membership, resources, information, rights, and responsibilities; vulnerable absent positions and the effects of restoration cannot be omitted.","caption":"Based on groups three to five of A.2."}],"diagramBrief":"Two diagrams separately show route distinctions and six types of boundary interfaces; openness/closure is not ranked as good/bad. First check particular objects and consequences.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["No actual channel-effect test; teaching before/after proportions show only attribution gaps.","Specific boundary justification, jurisdictional authority, effects on excluded people, and actual restoration require field materials and independent procedural materials."],"taskId":"S-A-2","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§A.2, Chinese source lines 3634–3699","claim_support":["All 39 HV02 fields, R0 description/R1 channel effects, and ceilings."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-HV01","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§A.1, Chinese source lines 3568–3633","claim_support":["Candidate references may pass downstream with status retained."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-G2","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§4.4, Chinese source lines 610–619","claim_support":["G2 specified-channel effects and their limits."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-OECD","title":"OECD: Transport Bridging Divides, Chapter 2","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/transport-bridging-divides_55ae1fd8-en/full-report/component-5.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2020 report; read 2026-10-14","locator":"Measuring access to opportunities; definitions of three indicators; web page lines 3869–3877","claim_support":["A stop’s existence differs from access to opportunities; distance indicators and access within given times/modes are defined separately."],"limits":["The researcher read selected portions of this section. They prove no causal effect of a boundary change and do not measure each user’s language, cost, bodily, or safety barriers."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"A.2　边界与接口（HV02）; Chinese source lines 3866–3949; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-A-2/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-2/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-A-2/source/S-A-2.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-2/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"dcfa542d0c9f78ebd9819e43e518c5fd7a66a20d4cfd2e2b849de3083116cc14","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-a/a-02.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-a/a-02.md"}`),d={name:"en/theory/appendix-a/a-02.md"};function l(u,e,p,f,h,m){const t=n("SectionLesson");return a(),o("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=s("",1)),r(t,{section:"A.2"}),e[1]||(e[1]=c("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const y=i(d,[["render",l]]);export{b as __pageData,y as default};
